    <article class="content-panel">
      <h1>WebView vs Electron vs Tauri: Which Wrapper Should You Use?</h1>
      <p>Every "website to app" tool is really a <em>wrapper</em>: a native shell that renders your site using a browser engine. Android WebView, Electron, and Tauri are the three wrappers that matter — and they differ substantially in size, speed, and capability. Here's how to think about them, and how SiteToApp picks the right one for each platform.</p>

      <h2>Android WebView (the APK wrapper)</h2>
      <p>On Android, the wrapper is a small native Java <code>Activity</code> that hosts a <code>WebView</code> — the same engine Chrome uses, already installed on every Android device. Because the engine ships with the OS rather than bundled with your app, the resulting APK is roughly <strong>30–60 KB</strong>. That's not a typo — just kilobytes, not megabytes.</p>
      <p>You still get pull-to-refresh, splash screens, offline fallback, and full access to Android system APIs. Apps download in seconds, start instantly, and sail through Play Store policy review. This is what SiteToApp's <a href="/android">Android builder</a> always generates — there's no heavier alternative on mobile.</p>

      <h2>Electron (the Windows default)</h2>
      <p>Electron bundles a complete Chromium + Node.js runtime inside your app. That means every desktop feature you can imagine works: native file system access, background workers, auto-updates, system tray integration, and a massive library of npm packages designed specifically for Electron. The installer comes in at <strong>65–90 MB</strong> because it's shipping its own browser — but that browser is 100% under your control, independent of whatever the user has installed.</p>
      <p>Cold starts are slower while Chromium boots, and RAM use is higher than a native app. For most use cases that's acceptable. Choose Electron when your site needs Node.js features, when you want maximum ecosystem support, or when compatibility with every Windows version matters. It's the SiteToApp <a href="/windows">Windows builder</a> default for good reason.</p>

      <h2>Tauri (the lean Windows alternative)</h2>
      <p>Tauri takes a different approach: a Rust shell that delegates all rendering to the system's WebView engine (Edge WebView2 on Windows 10/11). The result is an installer of <strong>5–10 MB</strong> — roughly ten times smaller than Electron — with near-instant cold start and a fraction of the memory footprint.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is a narrower ecosystem: no Node.js runtime, so some native integrations require Rust or OS-level code. For the overwhelming majority of website wrappers, that's not a constraint. Choose Tauri when size and performance are the top priority and your site doesn't need Node.js. SiteToApp offers it as a one-click alternative in the <a href="/windows">Windows builder</a>.</p>

      <h2>Head-to-head comparison</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>App size:</strong> WebView (≈0.05 MB) → Tauri (≈5–10 MB) → Electron (≈65–90 MB)</li>
        <li><strong>Cold start:</strong> WebView and Tauri are near-instant; Electron pays a Chromium boot cost of 1–3 seconds.</li>
        <li><strong>RAM use:</strong> WebView is lightest (shared OS engine); Tauri is lean; Electron is heaviest (dedicated Chromium).</li>
        <li><strong>Native feature access:</strong> Electron has the deepest integration via Node.js; Tauri and WebView cover the standard API surface well.</li>
        <li><strong>Ecosystem &amp; plugins:</strong> Electron has the largest npm ecosystem; Tauri's Rust ecosystem is growing rapidly; WebView is fully platform-native.</li>
        <li><strong>Update story:</strong> All three can be rebuilt and redistributed freely; Electron has the most mature auto-update libraries (electron-updater).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Which should you choose?</h2>
      <p>The answer usually follows a simple decision tree:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Android target?</strong> WebView, always. It's the only option in the SiteToApp Android builder, and it's the right one — tiny, fast, and Play-Store-ready.</li>
        <li><strong>Windows — need Node.js or exotic native APIs?</strong> Choose Electron. Its ecosystem has a package for almost everything.</li>
        <li><strong>Windows — prioritising a lean, modern installer?</strong> Choose Tauri. For most website wrappers, the system WebView is more than sufficient.</li>
        <li><strong>Unsure?</strong> Start with Tauri in the <a href="/windows">Windows builder</a>. If you hit a limitation, switching to Electron is a one-click change.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Ready to build? Try the <a href="/android">Android builder</a> or the <a href="/windows">Windows builder</a>, or check the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> for common questions about wrappers, signing, and publishing.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
